WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.820
When it comes to television, getting a pilot
greenlit by the network is usually good news

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.250
for everyone involved.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:10.019
Unfortunately for these stars, though, their
appearances in the first round of filming

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:14.820
for an eventual hit show didn't pan out because
they were replaced before their series went

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.340
into full production.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:21.020
Whether it was professional conflicts or personal
matters, these actors lost what became pretty

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.810
iconic small screen roles, despite filling
them first.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.920
Here are some TV characters who were recast
after the pilot.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.150
The Incredible Hulk

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:35.020
Lou Ferrigno became a household name thanks
to his successful run as the mean green monster

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.920
in the classic '70s TV series, but it was
originally Richard Kiel who'd signed up to

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.120
star as the Hulk.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.600
The eventual James Bond villain was hired
to film two movies of the week that were to

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.159
serve as the pilot for the proposed series.

00:00:47.159 --> 00:00:51.960
However, Kiel, who was blind in one eye, was
uncomfortable in the full contact lenses that

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.800
the part required, and also didn't like the
green body paint.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.629
Producers also felt that Kiel didn't fit perfectly
in the part, since even though he was over

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:02.710
seven-feet tall, he didn't boast a bodybuilder
physique.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:07.590
The role was eventually given to Ferrigno,
but Kiel's turn is still briefly visible in

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.230
the scene in which Hulk saves a young girl
from drowning.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.230
Danny Tanner

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:14.690
Full House almost had a different dad.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:18.840
John Posey was initially brought on to play
the role of Danny Tanner on the popular sitcom,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.570
but he was replaced by comedian Bob Saget
after filming the pilot.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:26.460
Posey had just two credits to his name when
he signed on for the pilot and said he was

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.480
actually on his way from Atlanta to Los Angeles
to work on the series when he got the call

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.340
saying he had been replaced.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.690
Show creator Jeff Franklin wanted either Saget
or Paul Reiser from the beginning, but they'd

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.280
both been tied up with other potential shows.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.450
"Neither Paul nor Bob were interested.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.350
We went through a casting process, we ended
up casting a complete unknown."

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.190
When Saget became available, however, Franklin
decided to give Posey the ax.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:55.060
"I just, I don't know, as soon as I created
this character, I thought of Bob."

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.870
Posey still went on to have a very successful
career as a small screen guest star.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.030
However, he's nowhere near as well-known as
the man who became America's dad on the beloved

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.590
sitcom.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.310
Sookie St. James

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:10.479
Before she became such a big screen sensation,
Melissa McCarthy was perhaps best known for

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:14.700
her role as the sweet Stars Hollow chef Sookie
St. James on Gilmore Girls.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.590
However, the actress almost wasn't chosen
to wear Sookie's signature smock.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:23.170
In the series' unaired pilot, comedic actress
Alex Borstein originally appeared in the role,

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:26.010
but had to back away due to her contract with
MADtv.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.260
Borstein still got to appear on the show,
just in a much smaller role.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.730
Instead of playing Sookie, the actress made
a couple of cameos as the sharp-tongued harp

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.830
player Drella, and then reemerged as the family's
stylist Miss Celine.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.550
Willow Rosenberg

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.560
Buffy the Vampire Slayer's original pilot
has actually surfaced, which means that fans

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.890
can go back and watch the original Willow
Rosenberg in action to see why another actress

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:50.340
was brought in to play her.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.780
Alyson Hannigan would famously go on to take
over the part, but it was Riff Regan who starred

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:59.319
in the short pilot creator Joss Whedon [made
for the network in a bid to get the show picked

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:00.319
up.

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:03.500
The network had a few issues with the pilot,
and one of the big points was Regan, who played

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:07.220
a much more quiet and subdued version of the
character than the one fans would come to

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.740
know in the show's full run.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.440
Daenerys Targaryen

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.880
Emilia Clarke's portrayal of Khaleesi is a
major part of why Game of Thrones is one of

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.410
the most popular shows on TV, but she wasn't
the first actor brought on to play the part

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.020
of the Mother of Dragons.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.200
That honor instead went to Tamzin Merchant,
an English actress known for her role in The

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.200
Tudors.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.000
While we don't know exactly why Merchant was
let go after the pilot, Game of Thrones showrunners

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.260
Dan Weiss and David Benioff have been pretty
open in their discussions about the shortcomings

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.500
of the original pilot, which had to be almost
completely re-shot so that it could eventually

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.990
become such a massive cultural phenomenon.

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.340
"So, thank you."

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.489
Catelyn Stark

00:03:45.489 --> 00:03:49.560
Daenerys wasn't the only Game of Thrones role
to be recast after the pilot.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.510
Actress Jennifer Ehle was initially set to
play Stark family matriarch Catelyn, but she

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.760
ended up dropping out of the role, which eventually
went to Michelle Fairley.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.700
Ehle stepped away from the role because it
involved a seven-year contract and, at the

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.240
time, her daughter was just a few months old.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.400
The show's producers ended up having Fairley
reshoot all of the scenes involving Ehle,

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.690
and the rest is history.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.120
Jenna Maroney

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.269
Jane Krakowski earned four Emmy Award nominations
for her role as 30 Rock's resident diva Jenna

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:20.970
Maroney, but it turns out that the character
was originally supposed to be a little different.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:25.090
Creator Tina Fey originally cast her former
Saturday Night Live co-star Rachel Dratch

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:30.740
in the role, but the part was given to Krakowski
after NBC saw the show's unaired pilot.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.440
Dratch's version of the role was still Liz's
best friend and the star of The Girlie Show,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.630
but the series would've originally focused
more on sketches.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:44.470
Dratch was later told the series style was
moving more toward that of a sitcom, meaning

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.640
that they needed an actress who could fit
that bill.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.530
Naturally, she was upset about the decision.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:53.520
"When you think about planning your SNL exit,
you don't think, 'I know, I'll leave and the

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.380
have a big public face plant."

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.540
But Dratch did go on to hold a number of other
roles on the show, including the overwhelmed

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:04.380
cat wrangler Greta, the Happy Days-obsessed
cleaning lady Maria, and the imaginary Blue

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:05.770
Man.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:06.770
Sarah Braverman

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:12.170
After becoming America's hippest mom Lorelai
on Gilmore Girls, Lauren Graham again captured

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:16.710
the hearts of small screen audiences everywhere
as Sarah Braverman on NBC's Parenthood.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:21.479
However, ER star Maura Tierney originally
occupied that role before having to drop out

00:05:21.479 --> 00:05:25.680
under terrible circumstances - after filming
the pilot, she learned that she had breast

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.680
cancer.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.860
The show's producers tried to accommodate
her treatments by pushing back production

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.260
and sending the show's premiere to mid-season,
but, in the end, it was too much for her and

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.680
she had to leave the show.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.430
In her place, Graham was brought on to take
over the part, and after Tierney recovered,

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.960
she went on to nab an award-winning role on
the hit Showtime series The Affair.

